<!-- 3. CHALLENGE -->
<section class="section white slide-page" id="challenge">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">01 · Product Context</span><h2>The problem was bigger than building 12 applications</h2></div>
      <p>Banks already operated within complex technology environments. The product therefore had to support domain-specific workflows while remaining configurable, integration-ready, migration-aware, controlled and implementable at client sites.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="story-grid">
      <div class="story reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">The product challenge</span>
        <h3>One platform had to serve very different banking jobs.</h3>
        <p>Lending, AML, cards, reconciliation, treasury and audit do not share the same users or workflows. The product challenge was to find the reusable platform patterns without flattening the domain complexity.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Existing bank applications meant coexistence and integration mattered from day one.</li>
          <li>Client-specific workflows required configuration rather than a rigid one-size-fits-all product.</li>
          <li>Regulated processes required roles, validations, approvals, evidence, traceability and reporting.</li>
          <li>Legacy data required field mapping, transformation, validation and post-migration checks.</li>
          <li>Deployment success required training, documentation and L1/L2 support readiness.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="story reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Product thesis</span>
        <h3>Standardise the platform. Configure the institution.<br/>Integrate the ecosystem.</h3>
        <p>The suite was approached as a common product foundation with modular domain applications—not as 12 disconnected projects.</p>
        <div class="story-flow">
          <div class="story-step"><b>Standardise</b><span>Roles, controls, workflow patterns, reporting conventions.</span></div>
          <div class="story-step"><b>Configure</b><span>Institution-specific business rules and journeys.</span></div>
          <div class="story-step"><b>Integrate</b><span>Bank systems, payment rails and external services.</span></div>
          <div class="story-step"><b>Adopt</b><span>Migration, deployment, training and support.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="challenge-response">
        <div><b>Configure</b><span>Institution-specific journeys without rebuilding the product.</span></div>
        <div><b>Integrate</b><span>Existing bank systems, payment rails and external services.</span></div>
        <div><b>Control</b><span>Roles, validations, approvals, evidence and traceability.</span></div>
        <div><b>Implement</b><span>Migration, deployment, training and support readiness.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 4. STRATEGY -->
<section class="section slide-page" id="strategy">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">02 · Product Strategy</span><h2>From application list to a reusable banking product ecosystem</h2></div>
      <p>The strategy connected platform architecture, domain product design, implementation realities and client adoption. Each decision was intended to reduce repeated effort while preserving the flexibility banks needed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="strategy reveal">
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#9b8cf5"><div class="num">01</div><h3>Common platform foundation</h3><p>Define reusable roles, permissions, workflow states, validations, reporting patterns and auditability before multiplying application complexity.</p></div>
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#73cbb4"><div class="num">02</div><h3>Configurable institution model</h3><p>Separate reusable product behaviour from bank-specific configuration so workflows can adapt without turning every implementation into a new product.</p></div>
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#7eb5ed"><div class="num">03</div><h3>Integration-first product design</h3><p>Identify systems of record, API contracts, authentication, failures, retries, reconciliation and hand-offs while journeys are being designed.</p></div>
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#efb785"><div class="num">04</div><h3>Controls by design</h3><p>Translate business and regulatory requirements into roles, maker-checker patterns, approval states, validations, evidence and reports.</p></div>
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#f49dbd"><div class="num">05</div><h3>Migration-aware product design</h3><p>Treat existing data as part of the product problem: assess, map, transform, validate, migrate and verify after migration.</p></div>
      <div class="strategy-card" style="--tone:#9dcbed"><div class="num">06</div><h3>Two-phase roadmap</h3><p>Develop the first six applications during the initial six months, then select and build the remaining six using the foundation and learnings from phase one.</p></div>
