      <div class="lesson"><strong>02</strong><h3>Integration is part of the UX</h3><p>Timeouts, invalid data, duplicates, retries, failure ownership and recovery paths must be designed as product behaviour.</p></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>03</strong><h3>Controls are product features</h3><p>In regulated workflows, who acted, under which role, with what evidence and what output is central to the product.</p></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>04</strong><h3>Enterprise UX is clarity</h3><p>Users need to see current state, owner, next action, exception reason and downstream effect without ambiguity.</p></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>05</strong><h3>Implementation belongs in product</h3><p>A feature is not truly successful if migration, integration, deployment, training or support prevents adoption.</p></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>06</strong><h3>Senior PM impact is cross-system</h3><p>End-to-end ownership connects strategy, technology, teams, client constraints, quality, release and measurable outcomes.</p></div>
